    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0,
      type: "MCQ",
      text: `<p></p> <p>Let \\(\\mathrm{G}=(\\mathrm{V}, \\mathrm{E})\\) be a simple, connected, <strong>non-Eulerian</strong> graph. We want to construct a graph \\(\\mathrm{H}\\) (not necessarily simple) that is <strong>Eulerian</strong> and that contains \\(\\mathrm{G}\\) as a subgraph. Consider the following possible construction processes:<br/><br/>(I) Add a new vertex, connecting it to every vertex of \\(\\mathrm{G}\\) by an edge.<br/><br/>(II) Add a new vertex, connecting it to every vertex of <strong>odd degree</strong> in \\(\\mathrm{G}\\) by an edge.<br/><br/>(III) Create a new copy \\(\\mathrm{G'}\\) of graph \\(\\mathrm{G}\\) and add an edge connecting each pair of corresponding vertices.<br/><br/>(IV) Duplicate all edges of \\(\\mathrm{G}\\).<br/><br/>(V) Add edges to \\(\\mathrm{G}\\) until the <strong>complete graph</strong> with \\(\\mathrm{|V|}\\) vertices is formed. </p> <p><br/>Which of the above processes <strong>always</strong> correctly constructs the graph \\(\\mathrm{H}\\)? </p> <p></p>`,
