      type: "MCQ",
      text: `<p></p> <p>The diagram below illustrates Router \\(\\mathrm{R} 1\\) sending a datagram to host \\(\\mathrm{H}\\) through Router \\(\\mathrm{R} 2\\). </p> <p style="text-align:center"><img alt="" height="87" src="js/questions/fst-mock-test-2/10.png" width="391"/></p> <p>Link \\(\\text{L}_1\\) only permits a \\(\\text{MTU}\\) of \\(1500\\) bytes. Link \\(\\text{L}_2\\) only permits a \\(\\text{MTU}\\) of \\(1100\\) bytes. ( \\(\\text{MTU}\\)= Maximum Transfer Unit)<br/> \\(\\text{A}\\) is an IP datagram which </p> <ol start="1" style="list-style-type:upper-roman"> <li>Has size \\(4000\\) bytes (the size of a datagram includes its header) </li> <li>Is not using any of the option fields in its header.</li> </ol> <p>Because \\(\\text{A}\\) is larger than the \\(\\text{MTU}s\\) of Links \\(\\text{L}_1\\) and \\(\\text{L}_2, \\text{A}\\) must be fragmented as it is sent from \\(\\mathrm{R} 1\\) to \\(\\mathrm{H}\\). Assume that all datagrams sent are received successfully. </p> <p>In order for Host \\(\\mathrm{H}\\) to receive the data in \\(\\mathrm{A}\\), How many IP datagrams does \\(\\mathrm{H}\\) receive? </p> <p></p>`,
