      solution: `<img src='js/questions/fst-mock-test-2/51.png' alt='solution' style='max-width:100%; border-radius: 8px;'>`
    },
    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0.67,
      type: "MCQ",
      text: `<p></p> <p>Let \\(S=\\{a, b\\}\\) be an alphabet, with total order \\(a&lt;b\\). Let \\(\\Sigma=\\text{S}^*\\) be the set of all strings over \\(\\text{S};\\) for \\(w=s_1 s_2 \\ldots s_n \\in \\Sigma\\) we write \\(\\ell(w)=n\\), and for \\(1 \\leqslant r \\leqslant n=\\ell(w)\\) we write \\(w_r=s_1 s_2 \\ldots s_r\\). Denote by \\(\\varepsilon\\) the unique word of \\(\\Sigma\\) such that \\(\\ell(\\varepsilon)=0\\), the null string. Conventionally \\(w_0=\\varepsilon\\) for all words \\(w \\in \\Sigma\\). </p> <p>Define relation \\(\\prec\\) on \\(\\Sigma\\) as follows: </p> <p>Let \\(v, w \\in \\Sigma\\), and \\(n=\\min \\{\\ell(v), \\ell(w)\\}\\). Let \\(r=\\max \\left\\{i \\mid v_i=w_i\\right\\} \\leqslant n\\). </p> <p>Then \\(v \\prec w\\) if:<br/> <span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ \\begin{array}{ccl} \\text { either } &amp; (i) &amp; \\ell(v)=r \\\\ \\text { or } &amp; (i i) &amp; v_{r+1}=v_r a, \\quad w_{r+1}=w_r b, \\quad \\text { where } v_r=w_r . \\end{array} \$\$ </p> <p>Which of the following is true for \\((\\Sigma,\\prec)?\\) </p> <p></p>`,
      image: "",
      options: [
        `\\(\\prec\\) is reflexive and symmetric.`,
        `\\(\\prec\\) is reflexive But not symmetric.`,
        `\\(\\prec\\) is not reflexive and not symmetric.`,
        `\\(\\prec\\) is symmetric But not reflexive.`,
      ],
      answer: "B",
      solution: `<img src='js/questions/fst-mock-test-2/52.png' alt='solution' style='max-width:100%; border-radius: 8px;'>`
    },
    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0.67,
      type: "MCQ",
      text: `<p></p> <p>Consider a relation \\(\\mathrm{R}\\) with \\(2 d\\) attributes \\(\\left(d\\right.\\) is an integer \\(\\geq 2\\) ) that are named \\(\\text{A}_1, \\text{A}_2, \\ldots, \\text{A}_{2 d}\\). There is a set \\(\\mathrm{F}\\) of \\(2 d \\; \\mathrm{FDs}\\) on \\(\\mathrm{R:} \\text{A}_i \\rightarrow \\text{A}_{1+\\langle i+1) \\bmod 2 d}\\) , for \\(i=1 \\ldots 2 d\\). Here, mod is the modulo operator (remainder after integer division, e.g., \\(17 \\bmod 5=2\\) ). For example, suppose \\(d=2\\), the attributes are \\(\\text{A}_1, \\text{A}_2, \\text{A}_3, \\text{A}_4\\) and \\(\\mathrm{F}=\\left\\{\\text{A}_1 \\rightarrow \\text{A}_3, \\text{A}_3 \\rightarrow \\text{A}_1, \\text{A}_2 \\rightarrow \\text{A}_4, \\text{A}_4 \\rightarrow \\text{A}_2\\right\\}\\) . </p> <p>What is the number of candidate keys in \\(\\mathrm{R?}\\) </p> <p></p>`,
      image: "",
      options: [
