    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0,
      type: "NAT",
      text: `<p>In the context of the pipeline, 'Predict-not-taken' is a method of branch prediction in which the hardware assumes that the next instruction to be executed will be the instruction following the branch instruction in memory. This (possible) next instruction is placed in the pipe and execution begins. If after the branch is finished executing, it is found that we really wanted to take the branch, then the (possible) next instruction that we had been executing is thrown out by the hardware, and we begin executing the correct instruction, the branch target. Consider two different \\(5\\)-stage pipeline machines \\(\\text{(IF ID EX MEM WB).}\\) The first machine \\(\\mathrm{M}\\) resolves branches in the \\(\\mathrm{ID}\\) stage, uses one branch delay slot, and can fill \\(80 \\%\\) of the delay slots with useful instructions. The second machine \\(\\mathrm{N}\\) resolves branches in the \\(\\mathrm{EX}\\) stage and uses a Predict-not-taken scheme. Assume that the cycle times of the machines are identical. Given that \\(35 \\%\\) of the instructions are branches, \\(25 \\%\\) of branches are taken, and that stalls are due to branches alone, what is the Speedup of machine \\(\\text{M}\\) over machine \\(\\mathrm{N}\\) (up to three decimal places)? (Speedup is the ratio of machine \\(\\mathrm{N}\\) running time to machine \\(\\mathrm{M}\\) running time.) </p>`,
      image: "",
      options: [
      ],
      answer: "1.098 :\n                    1.099",
      solution: `<img src='js/questions/fst-mock-test-2/58.png' alt='solution' style='max-width:100%; border-radius: 8px;'>`
    },
    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0,
      type: "NAT",
      text: `<p>In a \\(32\\text{-bit}\\) machine we subdivide the virtual address into 4 segments as follows: <span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ \\begin{array}{||c|c|c|c||} \\hline 8\\text{-bit} &amp; 4\\text{-bit} &amp; 8\\text{-bit} &amp; 12\\text{-bit} \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} \$\$We use a \\(3\\)-level page table, such that the first \\(8\\text{-bit}\\) are for the first level and so on. Physical addresses are \\(44\\) bits and page table entry size at every level is \\(8\\) bytes. How much minimum amount of memory (in Bytes) is consumed by the page table across all levels for a process that has \\(64 \\mathrm{~KB}\\) of memory starting at address \\(0?\\) </p>`,
