    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0,
      type: "MSQ",
      text: `<p></p> <p>A sender \\(\\mathrm{S}\\) and receiver \\(\\mathrm{R}\\) are connected over a network that has \\(\\mathrm{k}\\) links that can each lose packets. Link \\(i\\) has a packet loss rate of \\(p_i\\) in one direction (on the path from \\(\\text{S}\\) to \\(\\text{R}\\)) and \\(q_i\\) in the other (on the path from \\(\\mathrm{R}\\) to \\(\\text{S}).\\) Assume that each packet on a link is received or lost independent of other packets, and that each packet's loss probability is the same as any other's (i.e., the random process causing packet losses is independent and identically distributed). </p> <p>Suppose that the probability that a data packet does not reach \\(\\mathrm{R}\\) when sent by \\(\\mathrm{S}\\) is \\(p\\) and the probability that an \\(\\text{ACK}\\) packet sent by \\(\\mathrm{R}\\) does not reach \\(\\mathrm{S}\\) is \\(q\\). Which of the following are CORRECT? </p> <p></p>`,
