    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0,
      type: "MSQ",
      text: `<p></p> <p>Consider the arrangement of hosts \\(\\mathrm{H}\\) and routers \\(\\mathrm{R}\\) in the following figure. All links are full-duplex, and bandwidth given in diagram is on both ways.<br/> <img alt="" height="235" src="js/questions/fst-mock-test-2/18.png" width="552"/><br/> Let the bandwidths of all links (in both directions) at first level is \\(8 \\mathrm{~MBps}\\), at second level is \\(4 \\mathrm{~MBps,}\\) and at third level is \\(2 \\mathrm{~MBps}.\\) </p> <p>A router is congested if incoming packet rate is higher than outgoing packet rate. For example if \\(\\mathrm{H} 5, \\mathrm{H} 6\\) and \\(\\mathrm{H} 7\\) all of these wants to send packets to \\(\\mathrm{H} 8\\) then \\(\\mathrm{R} 7\\) will get congested because incoming rate will be \\(6 \\mathrm{~MBps}\\) and outgoing rate is \\(2 \\mathrm{~MBps}\\).<br/> <br/> Which of the following router(s) will never get congested for any traffic pattern? </p> <p></p>`,
