    {
      marks: 1,
      neg: 0.33,
      type: "MCQ",
      text: `<p> <meta/> </p> <p>Given positive integers \\( a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_k \\). </p> <p>For a positive integer \\( x \\) and \\( j \\le k \\), let \\( D(x, j) \\) denote the number of ways to write \\( x \\) as the sum of numbers chosen from \\( \\{a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_j\\} \\), where each number can be used at most once and order does not matter. </p> <p>For example, if \\( a_1 = 1, a_2 = 2, a_3 = 3 \\) and \\( a_4 = 4 \\), then there are exactly two ways to write \\( x = 5 \\) using \\( \\{a_1, a_2, a_3, a_4\\} \\), namely: </p> <p style="text-align:center;"> \\( 5 = 2 + 3 = 1 + 4 \\) </p> <p>Hence, \\( D(5,4) = 2 \\). </p> <p> </p> <p>For the purpose of this question, you do not need to worry about base cases.</p> <p>Which of the following recurrence relations correctly computes \\( D(x, j) \\)? </p> <p></p>`,
      image: "",
