      text: `<p></p> <p>A \\(\\text{3-ary}\\) boolean function is a function that takes three boolean arguments and produces a boolean output. Let \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) be \\(\\text{3-ary}\\) boolean functions. We say that \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) are neighbours if \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) agree on at least one input and disagree on at least one input: that is, there exist \\(x,\\: y,\\: z\\) such that \\(f(x, y, z) = g(x, y, z)\\) and \\(x',y',z'\\) such that \\(f(x',y',z') \\neq g(x',y',z')\\) .<br/><br/>Suppose we fix a \\(\\text{3-ary}\\) boolean function \\(h\\). How many neighbours does \\(h\\) have? </p> <p></p>`,
