    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0.67,
      type: "MCQ",
      text: `<p></p> <p>Let \\(M\\) be a Turing machine over the alphabet \\(\\Sigma\\). We denote by \\(\\operatorname{ACCEPT}(M)\\) the set of strings accepted by \\(M\\). A language \\(L \\subseteq \\Sigma^{*}\\) is called <strong>Turing-recognizable</strong> if there exists a Turing machine \\(M\\) such that \\(L=\\operatorname{ACCEPT}(M)\\). We use \\(\\operatorname{TR}(L)\\) to denote that the language \\(L\\) is Turing-recognizable. In this sense, analyze the following statements about two languages \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) over the alphabet \\(\\Sigma\\) : </p> <ol style="list-style-type:upper-roman"> <li>If \\(\\operatorname{TR}\\left(L_{1}\\right)\\) and \\(\\operatorname{TR}\\left(L_{2}\\right)\\), then \\(\\operatorname{TR}\\left(L_{1} \\cup L_{2}\\right)\\).<br/> </li> <li>If \\(\\operatorname{TR}\\left(L_{1}\\right)\\), then \\(\\operatorname{TR}\\left(\\Sigma^{*} \\backslash L_{1}\\right)\\) .<br/> </li> <li>If \\(\\operatorname{TR}\\left(L_{1}\\right)\\) and \\(\\operatorname{TR}\\left(L_{2}\\right)\\), then \\(\\operatorname{TR}\\left(L_{1} \\cap L_{2}\\right)\\).<br/> <ol style="list-style-type:upper-alpha"></ol> </li> </ol> <div>Which is correct?<br/> </div> <p></p>`,
