    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0.67,
      type: "MCQ",
      text: `<p></p> <p>According to the \\(\\textsf{IEEE}\\) standard, a \\(32\\)-bit, single-precision, floating-point number \\(\\mathrm{N}\\) is defined to be<br/><span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ \\text{N}=(-1)^\\text{S} \\times 1 . \\text{F} \\times 2^{\\text{E}-127} \$\$ <br/>where \\(\\mathrm{S}\\) is the sign bit, \\(\\mathrm{F}\\) the fractional mantissa, and \\(\\mathrm{E}\\) the biased exponent. </p> <p>A floating-point number is stored as \\(\\text{S : E : F},\\) where \\(\\text{S, E},\\) and \\(\\text{F}\\) are stored in \\(1\\) bit, \\(8\\) bits, and \\(23\\) bits, respectively. </p> <p>What is the decimal value of the floating-point number \\(\\textsf{C1E00000}\\) (hexadecimal notation)? </p> <p></p>`,
