      neg: 0,
      type: "MSQ",
      text: `<p> <meta/> </p> <p>An operating systems student is attempting reverse engineering of a machine whose page table was lost after a crash. Only a small log of recent address translations is available, and the student wants to reconstruct the missing page table entries from this behavior.</p> <p>The available address translation log is shown below.</p> <p><span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ \\begin{array}{c c c} \\text{Virtual address} &amp; &amp; \\text{Physical address} \\\\ \\hline \\text{0x1063} &amp; \\rightarrow &amp; \\text{0x2063} \\\\ \\text{0x584a} &amp; \\rightarrow &amp; \\text{0xe84a} \\\\ \\text{0x4dfe} &amp; \\rightarrow &amp; \\text{Invalid} \\\\ \\text{0x2a9f} &amp; \\rightarrow &amp; \\text{0x9a9f} \\end{array} \$\$ </p> <p>The system configuration is as follows.</p> <p><span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ \\text{Virtual address space} = 32\\text{ KB}, \\quad \\text{Physical memory} = 64\\text{ KB}, \\quad \\text{Page size} = 4\\text{ KB} \$\$ </p> <p>A simple linear page table is used, where each page table entry stores a valid bit and a physical frame number (PFN) and is written in the form \\( (\\text{Valid bit}, \\text{PFN}) \\). Let \\(\\text{PTE}\\) denote the page table implemented as an array, where \\(\\text{PTE}[i]\\) represents the entry corresponding to virtual page number \\(i\\). </p> <p>Based only on the above log, select all options that must be correct.</p> <p></p>`,
      image: "",
      options: [
        `\\(\\text{PTE}[1] = (1,\\,2)\\)<br/>`,
        `\\(\\text{PTE}[2] = (1,\\,\\text{0x9})\\) <br/>`,
        `\\(\\text{PTE}[4] = (0,\\,\\_)\\)<br/>`,
        `\\(\\text{PTE}[5] = (1,\\,\\text{0xE})\\)`,
      ],
      answer: ["A", "B", "C", "D"],
      solution: `<img src='js/questions/fst-mock-test-3/63.png' alt='solution' style='max-width:100%; border-radius: 8px;'>`
    },
    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0,
      type: "NAT",
      text: `<p></p> <p>Consider the following process-ordering diagram:</p> <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:10px;"> <tbody> <tr> <td style="width:45%; vertical-align:top; padding-right:20px;"><img alt="Process ordering" src="js/questions/fst-mock-test-3/4.png" style="max-width:100%;"/></td> <td style="width:55%; vertical-align:top;"> <p>The ordering constraints are:</p> <ul style="list-style-type:disc; padding-left:20px;"> <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;">A must finish before B starts</li> <li style="margin-bottom:10px;">A must finish before C starts</li> <li style="margin-bottom:10px;">B must finish before D starts</li> <li style="margin-bottom:10px;">C and D must both finish before E starts</li> </ul> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Each dependency of the form “X must finish before Y starts” is enforced using a semaphore.</p> <p>What is the minimum number of counting semaphores required to correctly enforce all the above ordering constraints?</p> <p></p>`,
      image: "",
      options: [
      ],
      answer: "3",
      solution: `<img src='js/questions/fst-mock-test-3/64.png' alt='solution' style='max-width:100%; border-radius: 8px;'>`
    },
    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0.67,
      type: "MCQ",
      text: `<p> <meta/> </p> <div style="max-width:900px; line-height:1.8; font-size:1.05em;"> <p>A sender \\(S\\) communicates with a receiver \\(D\\) using a TCP variant where duplicate ACKs are ignored and loss detection occurs only via timeout. The retransmission timeout is \\(\\mathrm{RTO}=3\\) RTTs. Transmission starts at \\(t=0\\) in slow start, packet numbering begins at \\(1\\), and the \\(14^{\\text{th}}\\) packet is lost during its first transmission. </p> <p>We assume continuous transmission at the sender: as soon as transmission of one window completes, transmission of the next window begins; i.e., the sender always has data to send.</p> <p>The slow-start threshold is \\(\\mathrm{ssthresh}=18\\) MSS. </p> <p>Packets sent at the start of each RTT are partially shown below:</p><span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ \\begin{array}{c|c} \\text{RTT start} &amp; \\text{Packets sent} \\\\ \\hline 1 &amp; 1 \\\\ \\hline 2 &amp; 2,\\;3 \\\\ \\hline 3 &amp; \\text{(Not given)} \\\\ \\hline 4 &amp; \\text{(Not given; packet }14\\text{ is lost)} \\\\ \\hline 5 &amp; ? \\end{array} \$\$ <p>Which of the following correctly represents the packets transmitted at the start of RTT \\(5\\)? </p> </div> <p></p>`,
      image: "",
      options: [
        `\\(16–27\\)`,
        `\\(16–29\\)`,
        `\\(14–27\\)`,
        `None of the above`,
      ],
      answer: "A",
      solution: `<img src='js/questions/fst-mock-test-3/65.png' alt='solution' style='max-width:100%; border-radius: 8px;'>`
    },
    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0,
      type: "MSQ",
      text: `<p> <meta/> </p> <p style="line-height:1.8;">Consider the statically routed IP network consisting of three routers as shown in the topology below. Router 3 is connected to an Ethernet network \\(128.100.11.0/24\\). The interfaces of each router are labeled as \\(I1, I2, I3\\). All routers use longest prefix matching. </p> <p style="text-align:center; margin:22px 0"><img alt="Statically routed IP network topology" height="192" src="js/questions/fst-mock-test-3/5.png" style="max-width:100%;" width="533"/></p> <p style="line-height:1.8;">The routing tables configured on the three routers are shown below.</p> <table cellpadding="0" style="width: 100%; border-spacing: 0px;"> <tbody> <tr valign="top"> <td style="padding-right:18px; width: 33%;"> <p><b>Router 1</b> <span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ \\begin{array}{c|c} \\textbf{Destination} &amp; \\textbf{Interface} \\\\ \\hline 128.100.11.32/27 &amp; I2 \\\\ 128.100.11.0/24 &amp; I3 \\\\ 0.0.0.0/0 &amp; I1 \\end{array} \$\$ </p> </td> <td style="padding:0 9px; width: 33%;"> <p><b>Router 2</b> <span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ \\begin{array}{c|c} \\textbf{Destination} &amp; \\textbf{Interface} \\\\ \\hline 128.100.11.32/27 &amp; I2 \\\\ 0.0.0.0/0 &amp; I1 \\end{array} \$\$ </p> </td> <td style="padding-left:18px; width: 33%;"> <p><b>Router 3</b> <span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ \\begin{array}{c|c} \\textbf{Destination} &amp; \\textbf{Interface} \\\\ \\hline 128.100.11.16/28 &amp; I2 \\\\ 128.100.11.0/24 &amp; I3 \\\\ 0.0.0.0/0 &amp; I1 \\end{array} \$\$ </p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p style="line-height:1.75;">Due to the above routing configuration, packets destined to some IP addresses enter a routing loop.</p> <p style="line-height:1.8;">Which of the following destination IP addresses, when received at Router 1, will result in packets looping indefinitely in the network?</p> <ol style="line-height:1.95; margin-left:0px; padding-left:20px; text-align:left;" type="A"></ol> <p></p>`,
      image: "",
      options: [
        `\\(128.100.11.10\\)`,
        `\\(128.100.11.18\\)`,
        `\\(128.100.11.25\\)`,
        `\\(128.100.11.40\\)`,
      ],
      answer: ["B", "C"],
      solution: `<img src='js/questions/fst-mock-test-3/66.png' alt='solution' style='max-width:100%; border-radius: 8px;'>`
    },
    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0,
      type: "MSQ",
      text: `<p> <meta/> </p> <p>Consider the sliding window packet transmission scenario shown below.</p> <table cellpadding="10" style="width: 100%; border-spacing: 0px;"> <tbody> <tr> <td style="width: 45%;" valign="top"> <ul> <li>Packet 2 is lost during transmission</li> <li style="line-height:normal;">At time t (indicated by the horizontal dashed line in the figure), the following ACKs are currently propagating from receiver to sender:<ul style="line-height:normal;"> <li>ACK0</li> <li>ACK1</li> <li>ACK3</li> </ul> </li> <li>No ACK has yet been received by the sender by time t</li> </ul> </td> <td align="center" style="width: 55%;" valign="top"><img alt="Sliding window packet transmission diagram" src="js/questions/fst-mock-test-3/6.png" style="max-width:100%; border:1px solid #ccc;"/></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Answer the following based only on future events at the sender after time t.</p> <p>Which of the following statements are correct? (Select all that apply.)</p> <p></p>`,
      image: "",
      options: [
        `If ACK0 is the next ACK received, the sender will advance its window and transmit packet 4.`,
