        `If ACK2 is the next ACK received (and ACK0 and ACK1 are lost), the sender will advance its window and transmit packet 6.`,
        `If ACK3 is the next ACK received (and ACK0 and ACK1 are lost), the sender will mark packet 3 as acknowledged but will not advance the window.`,
      ],
      answer: ["A", "D"],
      solution: `<img src='js/questions/fst-mock-test-3/67.png' alt='solution' style='max-width:100%; border-radius: 8px;'>`
    },
    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0,
      type: "NAT",
      text: `<p> <meta/> </p> <p style="line-height:1.7;">Given two <em>sorted</em> arrays \\(A[1..m]\\) and \\(B[1..n]\\), we want to find the \\(k^{\\text{th}}\\) smallest element in the concatenation of the two arrays. Assume \\(k &lt; m\\) and \\(k &lt; n\\). </p> <p style="line-height:1.7;">We are allowed unit-time random access to any indexed element of both lists.<br/>Assume that all \\(m+n\\) numbers are distinct. </p> <p style="line-height:1.7;">If the worst-case running time of the best algorithm is expressed as</p> <p style="text-align:center; line-height:1.7;"><span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ \\Theta\\!\\left(n^a\\log^b m + m^c\\log^d n\\right), \$\$ </p> <p style="line-height:1.7;">then the value of</p> <p style="text-align:center; line-height:1.7;"><span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ a + 10b + 100c + 1000d \$\$ </p> <p style="line-height:1.7;">is ________.</p> <p></p>`,
      image: "",
      options: [
      ],
      answer: "1010",
