    {
      marks: 2,
      neg: 0.67,
      type: "MCQ",
      text: `<p></p> <p>Assume we have two graphs \\(\\text{G}_1=\\left(\\text{V}_1, \\text{E}_1\\right)\\) and \\(\\text{G}_2=\\left(\\text{V}_2, \\text{E}_2\\right)\\). Also assume that we have \\(\\text{T}_1\\) which is a MST of \\(\\text{G}_1\\) and \\(\\text{T}_2\\) which is MST of \\(\\text{G}_2\\). Now consider a new graph \\(\\text{G = (V, E)}\\) such that \\(V=\\text{V}_1 \\cup \\text{V}_2\\) and \\(E=\\text{E}_1 \\cup \\text{E}_2 \\cup \\text{E}_3\\) where \\(\\text{E}_3\\) is a new set of edges that all cross the cut \\(\\left(\\text{V}_1, \\text{V}_2\\right)\\) </p> <p>Following is an example of what \\(\\text{G}\\) might look like. The dashed edges are \\(\\text{E}_3\\), the solid edges in \\(\\text{G}_1\\) (on the left) are \\(\\text{T}_1\\), and the solid edges in \\(\\text{G}_2\\) (on the right) are \\(\\text{T}_2\\). </p> <p style="text-align:center"><img alt="" height="256" src="js/questions/fst-mock-test-6/17.png" width="505"/></p> <p>Now assume we want to find a MST of the new graph \\(\\text{G}\\). Consider the following algorithm which tries to do this: </p> <p> </p> <p>Algorithm:<br/> Maybe-MST \\((\\left.\\text{T}_1, \\text{T}_2, \\text{E}_3\\right)\\)<br/> { </p> <ol start="1" style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"> <li> \\(\\text{e}_{\\min }=\\) a minimum weight edge in \\(\\text{E}_3\\) </li> <li> \\(\\text{T}=\\text{T}_1 \\cup \\text{T}_2 \\cup\\left\\{\\text{e}_{\\min }\\right\\}\\) </li> <li>return \\(\\text{T}\\) </li> </ol> <p>}</p> <p>Which of the following assertion is TRUE for the given algorithm?</p> <p> </p> <p></p>`,
